course: biology
lesson: 292
title: Dogs from wolves to best friends: living with dogs today
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The dog was the first animal people ever tamed, long before anyone farmed. Wolves that were less afraid came near camps for scraps, the tamest stayed and had puppies, and over thousands of years they became dogs that read our faces and follow our pointing fingers.
explain: Living with a dog means learning its language. A dog cannot say «leave me alone», so it says it with its body: it yawns, licks its lips, turns its head away, shows the whites of its eyes, tucks its tail, goes stiff and still, and only then growls.
explain: Most dog bites to children come from a dog they know, at home, when someone hugs it, kisses its face, or bothers it while it eats, sleeps or looks after puppies. Reading the warnings stops nearly all of them.
explain: If a strange dog comes running at you, do not run or scream, because running makes you look like prey. Stand still like a tree, fold your arms in, look away and wait; then back away slowly.
explain: Dogs also work for us: they guide blind people, warn deaf people about sounds and can even smell some illnesses. A dog in a working harness is on duty, so you do not stroke it.
fact: The dog was the first animal people tamed, more than 15,000 years ago; DNA shows dogs came from an old kind of wolf, not straight from today's grey wolves.
fact: In Siberia scientists bred only the tamest foxes for over 60 years; the foxes became friendly and wagged their tails, and some even got floppy ears and patchy coats.
fact: Dogs have a special eyebrow muscle, which wolves mostly lack, that lifts the inner eyebrow to make puppy eyes.
fact: Dogs vary in size more than any other kind of mammal, and small dogs usually live longer than big ones.
fact: Even young puppies can follow a human pointing finger, and dogs see blues and yellows well but not reds and greens.
fact: A dog warns before it bites: it yawns, licks its lips, turns its head away, shows the whites of its eyes, tucks its tail, freezes and then growls.
fact: A growl is a warning, not rudeness; a dog that is punished for growling may learn to bite without warning.
fact: Most bites to children come from a dog the child knows, often at home, when the dog is eating, sleeping, hurt or with puppies, or when it is hugged or kissed on the face.
fact: Before stroking a dog, ask the owner, let the dog sniff your closed hand, and stroke its side or chest rather than reaching over its head.
fact: If a strange dog comes at you, do not run or scream; stand still like a tree, arms folded in, look away and then back away slowly; if knocked down, curl into a ball and cover your neck and ears.
fact: Guide dogs help blind people, hearing dogs alert deaf people to sounds, some dogs warn of seizures or low blood sugar, and some can smell certain illnesses; a dog in a working harness should not be stroked or distracted.
fact: Hachiko, a dog in Japan, went to Shibuya station every day for nearly 10 years after his owner died, waiting for him.
fact: Dingoes came to Australia with people thousands of years ago, went wild, and are now top predators there; they howl but rarely bark.
fact: Many dogs and cats raised together are friends, and a dog chasing a cat is usually play or a hunting urge, not hate.
exercise: Which animal did people tame first of all? | The dog | The dairy cow | The horse | The house cat
exercise: How did wolves probably first come to live with people? | Less fearful wolves came near camps for scraps and the tamest stayed | People caught wolf cubs and fully trained them as pets in just one year | Wolves were bred from foxes by the first farmers | Hunters swapped fresh meat for wolf puppies at markets
exercise: What happened to the foxes bred for tameness in Siberia? | They became friendly, wagged their tails and some got floppy ears | They grew bigger and bigger, year after year, until they turned into real wolves | They stayed exactly as wild and fearful as before | They lost their tails and their fur
exercise: What lets a dog make puppy eyes that wolves mostly cannot? | A special eyebrow muscle | Much bigger, rounder eyeballs | Longer and darker eyelashes | Tears that make the eyes shine
exercise: Which kind of mammal varies most in size? | The dog | The horse | The mouse | The house cat
exercise: How young can dogs follow a human pointing finger? | Even as young puppies | Only after ten years of training | Only if they are wolves | Dogs can never do it at all
exercise: Why is a yellow tennis ball easy for a dog to spot on grass? | Dogs see yellows well but not reds and greens | Dogs see only black and white, and yellow looks bright | Dogs find balls only by the sound they make | Yellow balls smell stronger
exercise: Which of these is a dog's warning that it wants to be left alone? | Licking its lips, yawning and turning its head away | Rolling onto its back for a long tummy rub | Fetching a ball and dropping it right at your feet | A loose body and soft, waggy tail
exercise: What does a dog showing the whites of its eyes and freezing mean? | It is uneasy and may bite if bothered | It is very relaxed and about to fall asleep | It wants to play chase with you | It is hungry
exercise: Why should a dog not be told off for growling? | The growl is its warning, and without it a dog may bite with no warning | Growling dogs are always happy and only asking for a stroke | Telling off makes it bark louder | Growling is good for its throat and keeps its voice strong
exercise: Which dog bites children most often? | A dog they know, often at home | A wild wolf deep in the dark forest | A police dog at work chasing a thief | A guide dog in a harness in the street
exercise: When is it best to leave a dog alone? | When it is eating, sleeping, hurt or with puppies | When it brings you a toy | When it is wagging its tail and its body is loose | When its owner says you may stroke it gently
exercise: How should you first meet a friend's dog? | Ask the owner, let it sniff your closed hand, then stroke its side | Hug it round the neck straight away so it knows you are a friend | Put your face close to its face | Pat the top of its head quickly before it can move
exercise: A strange dog runs at you in the park. What is best? | Stand still like a tree, arms in, and look away | Run away as fast as you can to the nearest gate | Scream loudly and wave both arms over your head | Stare into its eyes
exercise: If a dog knocks you down, how should you protect yourself? | Curl into a ball and cover your neck and ears | Lie flat on your back and kick out with your feet | Grab its collar | Shout at it face to face to show who is boss
exercise: Why should you not stroke a guide dog in a harness? | It is working and must keep its mind on its owner | Guide dogs are dangerous and are trained to bite strangers | Guide dogs hate children | Its harness is electric and gives a little shock
exercise: What is Hachiko famous for? | Waiting at a station for nearly 10 years after his owner died | Being the very first dog ever sent up into space in a rocket ship | Finding lost climbers buried under snow | Winning the most races
exercise: What sound do dingoes make far more than pet dogs do? | Howling, as they rarely bark | A soft purring, like a cat | Meowing | A loud hissing, like a goose
test: «she's my nan's dog, she knows me, I can hug her face and she'll love it» What is right? | Most bites come from dogs children know, and face hugs scare many dogs | Dogs you know never bite, so hugging her face is totally fine | All dogs love face hugs | Only big dogs mind hugs, little ones always love a cuddle
test: «dog keeps yawning and licking his lips when my little brother sits on him, he's just tired lol» What is happening? | The dog is uneasy and warning; lift your brother off | He is sleepy and happy and likes being sat on by kids | He wants your brother to sit on him even more | He is hungry
test: «that dog's bolting at us, LEG IT» What is better? | Do not run; stand still like a tree and look away | Run, dogs are slow and give up after a few steps | Run and scream as loud as you can so the owner hears | Climb the dog
test: In Tom and Jerry Spike the bulldog chases Tom just because dogs hate cats. What is real? | Many dogs and cats raised together are friends; chasing is often play | All dogs hate all cats from the day they are born, so they always fight | Dogs chase cats only in winter | Cats always chase dogs first
test: In Scooby-Doo, Scooby talks and reads clues. What can real dogs really do? | Read our faces and follow pointing, but not talk | Talk in full sentences if trained from a puppy | Read books | Solve hard mysteries all on their own, like detectives
test: Count them: a guide dog leads a blind man, and a hearing dog walks beside a deaf woman. How many legs altogether? | 12 | 8 | 4 | 10
test: Count them: a dingo mother and 5 pups at a waterhole. How many ears? | 12 | 6 | 10 | 24
test: What would happen if a dog was told off every time it growled? | It might stop warning and bite without warning | It would never bite anyone ever again | It would start to purr | It would turn into a calm guide dog with time
test: «why was the Siberian fox thing a big deal, they're just foxes» What did it show? | Breeding the tamest animals changed their behaviour and even their looks | Foxes turned into real dogs, barking and all, in just one year | Foxes can never be tamed | Wild foxes already wag their tails
test: «saw a guide dog in the shop, just gave him a cuddle, he didn't mind» What is the problem? | A working dog must not be distracted from keeping its owner safe | Guide dogs bite anyone who touches them | Cuddles make guide dogs ill | There is no problem at all
test: «my Great Dane and my mate's chihuahua were born the same week, they'll die the same year» What is more likely? | The chihuahua will probably live longer | They will both die on exactly the same day | The Great Dane will live much longer, since big means strong | Size makes no difference at all to dogs' lives
test: Why do people say a dog in the house should have a quiet bed nobody bothers? | A dog that can rest safely is far less likely to snap | Dogs never sleep in houses | A quiet bed stops the dog growing too big for the house | Beds make dogs bark
exam: Identify the conduct recommended when a person is approached at speed by an unfamiliar dog. | Remaining motionless with arms withdrawn and gaze averted | Running away with the utmost haste | Shouting and waving the arms vigorously | Staring directly into the eyes of the animal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the circumstances of most dog bites to children. | They are inflicted chiefly by dogs known to the child, frequently within the home | They are inflicted chiefly by wild wolves encountered in forests and remote countryside | They occur exclusively in public parks | They are inflicted chiefly by assistance dogs on duty
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a growl? | It is a warning, the suppression of which may result in a bite without warning | It is an expression of contentment, comparable to the purring of the domestic cat | It signifies hunger exclusively | It is invariably followed by play
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an assistance dog wearing a working harness ought not to be stroked. | It is on duty, and distraction may endanger the person it serves | It is likely to transmit disease to any person who touches it | It is untrained in the company of children and will bite | The harness conducts electricity
